  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "note-jeppson",
      "type": "text_bubble",
      "position": {
        "x": -560,
        "y": 0
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 600,
      "style": {
        "width": 460,
        "height": 600
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Note",
        "title": "JEPPSON (1974) 7-PIPE NETWORK",
        "text": "Validation case: the 7-pipe network of R. W. Jeppson, Steady Flow Analysis of Pipe Networks: An Instructional Manual, Utah State University, 1974, Ch. V, example problems 1-3 (pp. 47-48). The same network with the pump is AFT Fathom Verification Case 63.\n\nData: all pipes Darcy-Weisbach with roughness 0.012 in; water at 70 \u00b0F; all junctions at 80 ft, the datum of this model, so the tank levels are the reservoir heads above it (20, 25 and 15 ft). Demand 2.0 cfs at junction 2 (a flow source with negative set flow). Pump: least-squares fit of Jeppson's curve h = -10.33 Q\u00b2 + 2.823 Q + 22.29 (ft, cfs) over 0.5-1.1 cfs, within 0.11 ft.\n\nCases:\n\u2022 Problem 2: pump supply (base). Published flows (cfs): P1 0.533, P2 0.662, P3 1.338, P4 0.699, P5 0.639, P6 -0.129, P7 0.828; junction pressures 8.10 / 0.56 / 7.32 / 8.28 psi.\n\u2022 Problem 3: three reservoirs, pump removed (isolated, bypass open). Published flows (cfs): P1 0.979, P2 0.680, P3 1.320, P4 0.527, P5 0.793, P6 0.300, P7 0.227.\n\nThe demand is drawn at junction 2, so pipes P2, P3 and P5 are drawn against the flow and show negative flows; compare magnitudes. WalFlow adds the reservoir entrance loss that Jeppson neglects, which explains most of the remaining difference (largest about 2 % on the smallest flows)."
      }
    },
